       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="Storage-Providers" title="Storage Providers">

    <title>
        Storage Providers
    </title>
    <secondary-label ref="advanced"/>

    <chapter id="technical-info" title="Technical Information">
        <p>Storage Providers allow developers to create different methods of
            storing Honeypot blocks should the built-in methods not be enough for their specific use case.</p>

        <p>There are three different types of Storage Provider in Honeypot: <code>REGION</code>, <code>PLAYER</code>,
            and <code>PLAYER_HISTORY</code>. Developers can create Storage Providers for as many or as few types as they
            wish, and one provider can support more than one type if desired.</p>

        <p>Honeypot only contains one native storage method for each type. Player information, player history,
            and Block Data (the location and action of Honeypot) are stored within the default SQLite stores.
            It is necessary to have different methods of storage allows server managers to pick what is best for
            them.</p>

        <p>The built-in SQLite Store has been architected to use an <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R*-tree">R*-Tree</a>
            data
            structure, a variant of an <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R-tree">R-Tree</a> data structure but with
            better query performance. In testing, Honeypot's DB architecture allowed it to search a
            2,000,000<!--suppress WrsMissingSpaceChecker --><sup>3</sup> block area
            within milliseconds (That's 8E18, or eight quintillion blocks).</p>

        <p>Because Honeypot can achieve such extreme query times without nearly any overhead, it's recommended that you
            stick with the native Honeypot storage methods unless you know you absolutely need something else.</p>

        <warning>Honeypot processes synchronously, meaning that it expects events and actions to be processed
            as they happen, not at some future point in time. Creating asynchronous storage providers will have
            unintended side effects, since Honeypot does not expect to deal with asynchronous objects such as
            <code>Future</code>'s.
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Creating and Registering Storage Providers" id="creating-registering-storage-providers">
        <p>To create a Storage Provider for one or more Store types, create a class and annotate it with the <code>@HoneypotStore</code>
            annotation. This annotation requires two arguments.</p>
        <deflist>
            <def title="name" id="name-def">
                <p>The name of the storage provider. This must be unique across all storage providers <i>regardless of
                    type</i>. For example, no two <code>REGION</code> may be called "mysql", even if one is a <code>REGION</code>
                    Store and the other is a <code>PLAYER</code> store.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="type" id="type-def">
                <p>The type or types this store supports. Can be a single item or a list of multiple</p>
                <code-block lang="java" src="DemoHoneypotStore.java"/>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>Notice that a Honeypot Store must implement the correct Interface for the type. Each type has a dedicated
            interface, and you
            <emphasis>must</emphasis>
            implement it.
        </p>
        <tip>Honeypot includes an annotation processor to assist with defining stores properly. It will fail your builds
            if you do not define a Store properly, so it's useful to ensure you've correctly implemented everything. To
            use it, <a
                    href="https://github.com/TerrorByteTW/Honeypot/tree/master/api">check out this link.</a></tip>

        <p>Once the storage provider is complete, you must register it in your <code>onLoad()</code> method.</p>

        <code-block lang="java" src="demoStorageProvider.java"/>

        <p>Honeypot will allow registrations of storage providers only at load time, so you cannot reload Honeypot using
            <code>/reload</code> or a plugin like PlugManX. To use the Storage Provider that was added, see
            <a href="Plugin-Config.topic" anchor="experimental-settings">Configuring Honeypot</a>. You must update
            the name of the storage method to what you set in the <code>@HoneypotStore</code> annotation.
        </p>

        <note>
            Believe it or not, unlike Behavior Providers, Storage Providers <i>can</i> be changed during server runtime
            with <code>/honeypot reload</code>. However, new ones cannot be registered, they can only be <i>changed</i>.
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Using Storage Providers" id="using-storage-providers">
        <p>The static <code>Registry</code> class provides a lot of functionality for working with Honeypot data without
            needing
            to know the inner workings of the storage provider.</p>
        <p>A getter exists for each Store type to get the currently selected Storage Provider.</p>
        <deflist>
            <def id="get-region-store">
                <title><code>Registry#getRegionStore()</code></title>
                <p>Gets the currently loaded Storage Provider for <code>REGION</code> data.</p>
            </def>
            <def id="get-player-store">
                <title><code>Registry#getPlayerStore()</code></title>
                <p>Gets the currently loaded Storage Provider for <code>PLAYER</code> data.</p>
            </def>
            <def id="get-player-history-store">
                <title><code>Registry#getPlayerHistoryStore()</code></title>
                <p>Gets the currently loaded Storage Provider for <code>PLAYER_HISTORY</code> data.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>If you need to be able to get a Store Provider by name and/or type, you may do so using the methods on the
            <code>StoreRegistry</code>.</p>
        <code-block lang="java"><![CDATA[
            // Get the currently selected Storage Manager Registry
            // Honeypot supports custom registries, but it is not documented in the Wiki. Check the JavaDoc to learn more.
            var registry = Registry.getStorageManagerRegistry();

            // Get a specific Storage Provider without knowing its type
            // Returns Optional<Object> since the type is unknown
            var someStore = registry.get("my-store");

            // Get a specific Storage Provider with a known type
            // Returns Optional<T> since it's not guaranteed to exist
            var someRegionStore = registry.get("my-region-store", RegionStore.class);

            // Get all Storage Providers of a given type
            // Returns List<T> as it filters registered Stores instead of attempting to get a specific one
            var allPlayerStores = registry.getByType(StoreType.PLAYER, PlayerStore.class);
        ]]></code-block>
    </chapter>

